{
  "dom_id": "step-8800",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Using the Property of a Bisection",
  "problem": "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle DAC?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)",
  "worked_solution": "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so $m\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DAC & =\\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{79}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={39.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
